So we wanted to make sure that our seasons on the show coordinated with what would actually

983
00:49:14.680 --> 00:49:17.460
be happening in Scranton, Pennsylvania at the time.

984
00:49:18.160 --> 00:49:21.580
And it was my idea to not come all the way out the door.

985
00:49:22.160 --> 00:49:24.440
I said, Pam doesn't have her coat on.

986
00:49:24.560 --> 00:49:29.340
I think if if this were really me, I would just sort of hover inside and kind of talk through

987
00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:31.060
a crack in the door when it's really cold.

988
00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:33.840
And I just love that that made that in.

989
00:49:33.960 --> 00:49:34.980
They liked that idea.

990
00:49:35.240 --> 00:49:38.800
And so that's why Pam doesn't come all the way outside to talk to Michael.

991
00:49:39.240 --> 00:49:40.400
Oh, I loved that.

992
00:49:40.540 --> 00:49:44.600
I didn't know that, but it felt very real to me that you'd be like, hey, idiot, come

993
00:49:44.600 --> 00:49:45.100
inside.

994
00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:48.040
Can I confess something?

995
00:49:48.360 --> 00:49:48.520
Yeah.

996
00:49:48.640 --> 00:49:51.960
I really love when I get to pretend to be cold.

997
00:49:52.660 --> 00:49:53.120
Really?

998
00:49:54.060 --> 00:49:56.660
Yeah, because I'm cold all the time.

999
00:49:56.720 --> 00:50:03.120
I feel like I have such a full suitcase of material to pull from when I need to be cold.

1000
00:50:03.500 --> 00:50:05.040
I'm cold constantly.

1001
00:50:05.280 --> 00:50:08.920
And so that's why I was like, oh, you need me to pretend to be cold?

1002
00:50:09.080 --> 00:50:10.060
Well, I'll tell you what I would do.

1003
00:50:10.060 --> 00:50:12.220
I wouldn't walk all the way outside without a coat on.

1004
00:50:12.740 --> 00:50:14.100
Let me throw that at you.

1005
00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:19.900
And there was like there was an episode a few episodes ago where we were all in the

1006
00:50:19.900 --> 00:50:20.380
parking lot.

1007
00:50:20.500 --> 00:50:21.300
We were pretending to be cold.

1008
00:50:21.440 --> 00:50:22.460
And I saw myself.

1009
00:50:22.560 --> 00:50:27.520
I kept putting my mouth down into the front of my coat and breathing into it because that's

1010
00:50:27.520 --> 00:50:28.680
something I do when I'm cold.

1011
00:50:29.040 --> 00:50:33.120
I'm noticing all of my cold detail acting and I'm really proud of it.

1012
00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:35.240
So I'm giving a little flex to it right now.

1013
00:50:35.380 --> 00:50:36.660
All your cold stick.

1014
00:50:36.940 --> 00:50:37.920
You've got your cold.

1015
00:50:37.920 --> 00:50:44.300
Yeah, now I own a sweatshirt that says always cold that my family gave to me as a gift because

1016
00:50:44.300 --> 00:50:46.160
I drive everyone crazy.

1017
00:50:46.160 --> 00:50:51.220
I sneak up behind and turn the thermostat off like I'm that person.

1018
00:50:52.020 --> 00:50:56.580
So, Jenna, I don't know if I'm great at pretending to be cold, but I'm with you.

1019
00:50:56.780 --> 00:50:59.240
I mean, you and I froze our butts off on that set.

1020
00:50:59.540 --> 00:51:01.400
So now we're at the luau.

1021
00:51:01.860 --> 00:51:03.700
Angela Martin is cutting up spam.

1022
00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:06.860
Because I guess that's all you could get.

1023
00:51:06.860 --> 00:51:11.380
You couldn't get a full pig from the petting zoo, as you had told Phyllis.

1024
00:51:11.540 --> 00:51:11.900
Oh, Lord.

1025
00:51:12.220 --> 00:51:13.540
Well, my dad loves spam.

1026
00:51:13.720 --> 00:51:16.940
So when I saw that, I just got tickled because it made me think of my dad.

1027
00:51:17.100 --> 00:51:22.300
My dad, whenever my mom was really in charge of all the cooking and meal prep when I was

1028
00:51:22.300 --> 00:51:24.760
growing up, unless it was barbecuing like they were so traditional.

1029
00:51:24.880 --> 00:51:26.360
My dad like manned the barbecue.

1030
00:51:26.760 --> 00:51:31.540
But if my mom and it was very rare, but let's say my mom woke up and she was like, oh, gosh,

1031
00:51:31.540 --> 00:51:34.180
I have a cold, I have a fever, you know, whatever.

1032
00:51:34.340 --> 00:51:39.600
One of those mornings where my dad had to take over, my dad would fry spam and we'd all

1033
00:51:39.600 --> 00:51:41.680
be like, ah, and he'd be like, you're going to have spam.

1034
00:51:41.800 --> 00:51:43.780
And then he would fry it up for breakfaSt.

1035
00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:46.400
And then if he had to make our lunch, he'd be like, well, we got leftover spam.

1036
00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:49.480
It was like, dad, so much spam.

1037
00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:50.540
What is spam?

1038
00:51:51.280 --> 00:51:53.100
I think spam's a lot of parts.

1039
00:51:53.960 --> 00:51:56.640
Does it have flavors in it?

1040
00:51:56.800 --> 00:51:58.260
Is it a specific flavor?

1041
00:51:58.740 --> 00:52:00.340
It's very salty.

1042
00:52:00.680 --> 00:52:02.800
Okay, wait, we're getting a message from Cody.

1043
00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:06.520
This is the benefit of zooming our podcaSt.

1044
00:52:06.740 --> 00:52:07.900
Our producer can pop on.

1045
00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:14.020
She is telling us that spam's basic ingredients are, quote, pork with ham added, salt water,

1046
00:52:14.160 --> 00:52:19.580
modified potato starch as a binder, sugar and sodium nitrate as a preservative.

1047
00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:25.000
There is also a natural gelatin that is formed during cooking in its tins on the production

1048
00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:25.380
line.

1049
00:52:25.560 --> 00:52:30.600
Cody's clarifying, ham is from the pig's hind leg while pork is any meat from pigs.

1050
00:52:30.880 --> 00:52:34.020
Wait, guys, the spam conversation is not over.

1051
00:52:34.020 --> 00:52:34.640
It's not over.

1052
00:52:34.640 --> 00:52:38.780
Because I know you were worried that we were stopping the spam conversation, but Sam just

1053
00:52:38.780 --> 00:52:42.380
threw down that there are 15 different flavors of spam.

1054
00:52:42.940 --> 00:52:46.020
Okay, well, that seems crazy.

1055
00:52:46.640 --> 00:52:48.500
It's what you're here for, guys.

1056
00:52:48.500 --> 00:52:49.720
It's what you're here for.

1057
00:52:49.820 --> 00:52:50.420
It's what you're here for.

1058
00:52:50.820 --> 00:52:51.600
All right.

1059
00:52:51.860 --> 00:52:55.180
Well, so yes, we're back to the luau in the warehouse.

1060
00:52:55.800 --> 00:52:57.680
Jim gives Karen some papers.

1061
00:52:57.940 --> 00:52:58.560
What are they, Jenna?

1062
00:52:59.060 --> 00:52:59.440
Yes.

1063
00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:04.220
He hands her a rental agreement for the apartment that is two blocks away from him.

1064
00:53:05.000 --> 00:53:07.240
And they're so happy and they make up.

1065
00:53:07.880 --> 00:53:13.920
You know, Angela, we got a lot of questions about why are they having this luau down in the

1066
00:53:13.920 --> 00:53:14.280
warehouse?

1067
00:53:14.280 --> 00:53:15.680
People are kind of confused.

1068
00:53:16.120 --> 00:53:22.020
And I think that's because there were a bunch of scenes that got deleted from this section

1069
00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:26.440
of the show that remind you that we're supposed to be doing inventory down there.

1070
00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:30.380
There are so many deleted scenes from the warehouse.

1071
00:53:31.020 --> 00:53:33.020
So there were all of these little vignettes.

1072
00:53:33.200 --> 00:53:39.200
One of the ones I loved was that Ryan is staring at a bunch of boxes and he has like a clicker

1073
00:53:39.200 --> 00:53:42.900
in his hand, like, you know, that you would check off like a barcode, like beep, beep.

1074
00:53:43.460 --> 00:53:45.500
And Roy walks up and goes, what's the problem?

1075
00:53:46.100 --> 00:53:50.220
And Ryan's like, well, we're seven boxes short of whatever yellow paper, whatever it was.

1076
00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:57.760
And Roy just takes the clicker from Ryan and then just scans into the air like seven, beep,

1077
00:53:57.960 --> 00:53:58.580
beep, beep, beep, beep, beep, beep.

1078
00:53:58.700 --> 00:53:59.460
And he goes, fixed.

1079
00:53:59.860 --> 00:54:03.380
So there's like, and Ryan's like, oh, so we're not doing it.

1080
00:54:03.420 --> 00:54:04.300
We're just fudging it.

1081
00:54:04.440 --> 00:54:08.540
But like there's all these different moments within the warehouse that are happening that

1082
00:54:08.540 --> 00:54:11.060
remind you that we're actually there to do inventory.

1083
00:54:11.060 --> 00:54:17.500
Then another deleted scene that happens is Meredith is doing inventory and she's trying to get to

1084
00:54:17.500 --> 00:54:19.300
one of the boxes on top of the shelf.

1085
00:54:19.500 --> 00:54:23.260
And she does that thing, you guys, I have done so many times, especially in a grocery

1086
00:54:23.260 --> 00:54:25.560
store when I can't reach something on the top.

1087
00:54:25.860 --> 00:54:32.580
I climb up the bottom of the shelf and she does this and the whole entire shelf comes crashing

1088
00:54:32.580 --> 00:54:34.700
down on her head with all the boxes.

1089
00:54:34.940 --> 00:54:36.660
She's completely buried.

1090
00:54:36.680 --> 00:54:40.460
If you watch the deleted scenes, you can't even see Kate anymore.

1091
00:54:40.460 --> 00:54:41.420
She's buried.

1092
00:54:41.680 --> 00:54:42.640
No one notices.

1093
00:54:42.920 --> 00:54:44.540
People just keep on business as usual.

1094
00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:48.680
And then you hear this muffled voice say, hello, I'm stuck.

1095
00:54:49.360 --> 00:54:49.800
Hello?

1096
00:54:51.080 --> 00:54:53.780
We got a fan question about this moment.

1097
00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:59.880
Jess Moore wrote in and said, did Kate Flannery do her own stunt in that deleted scene in the

1098
00:54:59.880 --> 00:55:00.340
warehouse?

1099
00:55:01.240 --> 00:55:05.080
Well, Jess, we reached out to Kate and this is what she had to say.

1100
00:55:05.480 --> 00:55:07.940
Hey, Office Ladies podcast fans.

1101
00:55:07.940 --> 00:55:11.260
It's me, Kate Flannery, Meredith from The Office.

1102
00:55:12.280 --> 00:55:14.940
Yes, I did a lot of my own stunts.

1103
00:55:15.060 --> 00:55:21.980
I kind of got the idea that as much physical comedy as I could do, I was going to do.

1104
00:55:21.980 --> 00:55:27.240
So I said yes to a lot of my stunts, even though I know that there was a stunt double there that

1105
00:55:27.240 --> 00:55:30.740
day, Debbie Evans, but they did not use her.

1106
00:55:31.780 --> 00:55:32.860
Happened several times.

1107
00:55:33.200 --> 00:55:36.400
So I think the stunt people still got paid.

1108
00:55:36.540 --> 00:55:38.540
And I never went to the hospital, so who cares, right?

1109
00:55:39.200 --> 00:55:41.300
But yeah, we sort of perfected things.

1110
00:55:41.480 --> 00:55:47.220
So by the time Meredith gets hit by the car in season four, we were really off to the races.

1111
00:55:47.480 --> 00:55:54.180
So this episode, this stunt was kind of the beginnings of my bravery.

1112
00:55:54.580 --> 00:55:55.540
So thanks for watching.

1113
00:55:56.440 --> 00:55:57.340
She's fearless.

1114
00:55:57.600 --> 00:55:58.200
She really is.

1115
00:55:58.240 --> 00:55:58.900
That is insane.

1116
00:55:59.140 --> 00:56:00.200
I mean, she is fearless.

1117
00:56:00.200 --> 00:56:07.460
If you go and watch that deleted scene, truly a whole entire shelf full of stuff falls on her

1118
00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:08.800
and you can't even see her.

1119
00:56:10.340 --> 00:56:11.100
She's fearless.

1120
00:56:11.580 --> 00:56:14.340
I would have been like, Debbie, I'm going to need you.

1121
00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:15.980
I'm going to need you in.

1122
00:56:16.180 --> 00:56:20.700
Debbie, get on in here because there is no way I would have done that.

1123
00:56:20.860 --> 00:56:21.280
No way.

1124
00:56:21.620 --> 00:56:23.380
I am too much of a scaredy cat.

1125
00:56:24.180 --> 00:56:27.700
You know what's interesting is that one of the things I really wanted to do on this show,

1126
00:56:27.700 --> 00:56:30.480
I don't know why, I think I think I'm a badass driver.

1127
00:56:30.700 --> 00:56:34.120
I'm probably not, but I always wanted to do all my driving stunts.

1128
00:56:34.240 --> 00:56:37.760
And when they let me, like, because, you know, Dwight and I have a few driving scenes,

1129
00:56:38.220 --> 00:56:39.340
I would get really excited.

1130
00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:40.820
Don't want to do those either.

1131
00:56:41.040 --> 00:56:43.880
I like it when they hook it up to a rig and pull the car.

1132
00:56:44.020 --> 00:56:46.560
I'm like, I don't want to drive this down the road while I'm acting.

1133
00:56:46.840 --> 00:56:47.900
No one wants that.

1134
00:56:48.100 --> 00:56:49.200
I've had to do that before.

1135
00:56:49.220 --> 00:56:50.500
I'm always terrified.

1136
00:56:52.320 --> 00:56:56.780
Well, one thing that did make it into the scene is this very odd moment of Daryl finding

1137
00:56:56.780 --> 00:56:57.720
his iPod.

1138
00:56:58.140 --> 00:57:00.660
And I want you to know that is not in the script.

1139
00:57:00.860 --> 00:57:03.320
That was an improvisation by Craig on the day.

1140
00:57:03.500 --> 00:57:09.720
He actually found an iPod deck and speaker somewhere back in the warehouse.

1141
00:57:09.720 --> 00:57:12.640
And he just did this bit and it made it into the show.

1142
00:57:12.700 --> 00:57:14.180
And it is so funny to me.

1143
00:57:14.380 --> 00:57:16.220
We got so much mail about it.

1144
00:57:16.320 --> 00:57:18.380
People were like, is there something I'm missing?

1145
00:57:18.680 --> 00:57:24.080
Is there a deleted scene from a previous episode where Daryl loses his iPod?

1146
00:57:24.080 --> 00:57:27.060
What is this product placement for iPod again?

1147
00:57:27.280 --> 00:57:29.720
No, it was just this random funny bit.

1148
00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:33.820
He does it with such like real enthusiasm.

1149
00:57:34.500 --> 00:57:38.580
Well, we had to be there pretending like we were going through boxes, guys.

1150
00:57:38.860 --> 00:57:42.560
So we were really doing stuff, which you don't really see.

1151
00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:44.020
It didn't really make it into the show.

1152
00:57:44.060 --> 00:57:49.620
But I think that he legitimately opened up a box and that was in there and he got all excited.

1153
00:57:49.620 --> 00:57:51.540
Yes, exactly.

1154
00:57:52.020 --> 00:57:56.920
Well, I have a little Angela Roy moment that happens right at the same time.

1155
00:57:57.220 --> 00:58:05.160
At 14 minutes, 44 seconds, you can see Angela starts to walk up to Roy and is about to like

1156
00:58:05.160 --> 00:58:06.000
go up to him.

1157
00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:09.400
And then Daryl yells, I found an iPod dock.

1158
00:58:09.560 --> 00:58:12.080
And I whip my head and I pivot the other way.

1159
00:58:12.240 --> 00:58:12.640
Wow.

1160
00:58:13.160 --> 00:58:14.640
You have to go and watch it.

1161
00:58:14.920 --> 00:58:18.100
More of the untold Angela Roy story.

1162
00:58:18.100 --> 00:58:22.180
And I'm wondering, I mean, what what was meant to happen there?

1163
00:58:22.320 --> 00:58:23.320
Like, it's not in the script.

1164
00:58:23.500 --> 00:58:24.460
It's not in deleted scenes.

1165
00:58:24.780 --> 00:58:29.740
But I'm sure I was given some direction maybe to go over and, you know, in the background sort

1166
00:58:29.740 --> 00:58:30.540
of talk to Roy.

1167
00:58:31.240 --> 00:58:34.460
But yeah, when Daryl yells, I turn around and go the other direction.

1168
00:58:34.720 --> 00:58:35.820
It's a fun little catch.

1169
00:58:36.100 --> 00:58:44.040
Well, then we see Pam is doing some inventory and Karen comes up to her and thanks her.

1170
00:58:44.040 --> 00:58:48.560
She says, thank you so much for talking sense into Jim.

1171
00:58:49.120 --> 00:58:50.840
Karen is so happy.

1172
00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:53.380
Her relationship is back on.

1173
00:58:53.640 --> 00:58:54.440
Everything's OK.

1174
00:58:55.000 --> 00:59:00.880
And then we cut to Pam just sobbing on a bench in the hallway.

1175
00:59:02.830 --> 00:59:09.270
And Angela, I watched this scene and it just brought tears to my eyes because I remember

1176
00:59:09.270 --> 00:59:12.690
shooting it so clearly, so clearly.

1177
00:59:12.730 --> 00:59:16.330
And we got a lot of questions about it.

1178
00:59:16.750 --> 00:59:24.050
So we got questions from Joe Salazar, Angie Letworth, Elisa Goldman, Jillian Riley and Charlie

1179
00:59:24.050 --> 00:59:24.930
Sparkenbaugh.

1180
00:59:25.190 --> 00:59:30.490
They wanted to know, how was it filming that iconic Pam and Dwight scene when Pam is crying?

1181
00:59:31.510 --> 00:59:34.790
Well, guys, this was like early on in the show.

1182
00:59:34.990 --> 00:59:40.010
And whenever we had these really emotional scenes, like that parking lot scene with Jim

1183
00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:44.650
and Pam, we would just kind of shut down everything.

1184
00:59:44.850 --> 00:59:51.970
And I remember that we shut down the set and we took hours prepping and shooting this crying

1185
00:59:51.970 --> 00:59:52.390
scene.

1186
00:59:52.630 --> 00:59:55.930
It was really important to Greg that I really be crying.

1187
00:59:56.590 --> 00:59:59.230
They did not want to put fake tears in my eyes.

1188
00:59:59.230 --> 01:00:00.870
He wanted me to really go there.

1189
01:00:01.330 --> 01:00:04.370
And this is a really big challenge for an actor.

1190
01:00:05.610 --> 01:00:09.330
And so what they did was they made everything really quiet.

1191
01:00:10.210 --> 01:00:12.750
And no one talked to me for about a half hour.

1192
01:00:12.890 --> 01:00:15.930
And I put on the saddest music I could find.

1193
01:00:16.090 --> 01:00:20.250
And it was sad because it was personal to me.

1194
01:00:20.410 --> 01:00:20.710
Right.

1195
01:00:20.710 --> 01:00:28.770
It was music that brought up memories of breakups, music that I listened to at different times

1196
01:00:28.770 --> 01:00:31.830
in my life when I was having a particularly hard time.

1197
01:00:32.070 --> 01:00:34.250
So it would bring up a lot of emotions.

1198
01:00:35.110 --> 01:00:40.730
And I had spent the previous week creating this playlist because I knew that it would invoke

1199
01:00:40.730 --> 01:00:41.550
a lot of emotion.

1200
01:00:41.550 --> 01:00:45.050
And I sat and I listened to it until I was crying.

1201
01:00:45.570 --> 01:00:50.170
And then Kelly, our first AD, came and took the headphones away from me.

1202
01:00:50.510 --> 01:00:52.150
And then they started rolling.

1203
01:00:52.750 --> 01:00:56.790
And that's how they were able to begin the scene with me crying.

1204
01:00:57.610 --> 01:01:01.970
Because when you play a scene, Angela, and you have dialogue and you have connection with

1205
01:01:01.970 --> 01:01:06.230
a person, a lot of times that will bring the emotion out of you.

1206
01:01:06.350 --> 01:01:10.970
But it's really hard to start a scene in a full cry because you would just have to get

1207
01:01:10.970 --> 01:01:11.690
yourself there.

1208
01:01:12.750 --> 01:01:13.970
No, that's so true.

1209
01:01:14.110 --> 01:01:18.430
It's actually really hard because you're basically taking yourself emotionally from zero to 60.

1210
01:01:19.390 --> 01:01:23.850
And I had to do a crying scene in a show called Haters Back Off that was like something

1211
01:01:23.850 --> 01:01:24.410
in the middle.

1212
01:01:24.790 --> 01:01:30.030
And I remember they let me sort of just step away from set to get myself there.

1213
01:01:30.150 --> 01:01:32.090
And once I was there, they started rolling.

1214
01:01:32.310 --> 01:01:35.910
I took this acting class and it was talking about emotional journeys.

1215
01:01:36.930 --> 01:01:43.710
And the acting teacher encouraged us to pick a song, one song, because sometimes on set,

1216
01:01:43.850 --> 01:01:46.970
you don't have the luxury of being able to go listen.

1217
01:01:47.130 --> 01:01:51.450
That really shows how much our show was taking care of you in that moment, I feel like.

1218
01:01:52.850 --> 01:01:57.630
But sometimes you only have a few minutes to reset yourself emotionally and to get to that

1219
01:01:57.630 --> 01:01:57.990
place.

1220
01:01:58.230 --> 01:02:03.770
So we took everyone picked one song and in your mind, you sing that one song.

1221
01:02:03.910 --> 01:02:07.850
And by the end of that song, you should be in that emotional place.

1222
01:02:08.270 --> 01:02:10.190
And it really worked for me.

1223
01:02:10.390 --> 01:02:14.970
And I find for myself now, I'm older now, guys, I'm 49.

1224
01:02:14.970 --> 01:02:18.290
And more life has happened to me.

1225
01:02:19.650 --> 01:02:24.410
And it's easier for me to get to that place than when I was a younger actor.

1226
01:02:24.890 --> 01:02:25.410
Yeah, same.

1227
01:02:26.110 --> 01:02:29.970
But music is a big way to trigger it for me.

1228
01:02:30.050 --> 01:02:31.750
And I think for a lot of actors.

1229
01:02:32.090 --> 01:02:35.870
I've created musical soundtracks for a lot of things I've done.

1230
01:02:36.070 --> 01:02:39.950
I had to do a play and I had to start out the play in a rage.

1231
01:02:39.950 --> 01:02:46.250
I had to start out very, very angry and I couldn't find a song that exactly triggered my anger.

1232
01:02:46.390 --> 01:02:48.210
I had a song that would kind of amp me up.

1233
01:02:48.330 --> 01:02:57.010
And then I realized I had some audio of an ex-boyfriend who I like really was pissed at

1234
01:02:57.010 --> 01:02:58.210
still to this day.

1235
01:02:58.610 --> 01:03:05.690
I found this audio tape and I played his voice and it made me so damn mad that I would you

1236
01:03:05.690 --> 01:03:07.710
can hear me right now just thinking about it.

1237
01:03:07.710 --> 01:03:12.650
It made me so damn mad that I could just walk out on stage and start screaming at the other

1238
01:03:12.650 --> 01:03:16.010
actor on stage because just his voice triggered me.

1239
01:03:16.410 --> 01:03:16.510
Wow.

1240
01:03:16.810 --> 01:03:20.210
So I think that, you know, music, something you can listen to.

1241
01:03:21.670 --> 01:03:23.050
Yeah, that works.

1242
01:03:23.390 --> 01:03:24.550
Yeah, I think that goes.

1243
01:03:24.710 --> 01:03:29.430
I mean, you guys listening, you can I know there are songs that take you right back to a moment

1244
01:03:29.430 --> 01:03:29.970
in your life.

1245
01:03:30.130 --> 01:03:32.650
That's that's the power and beauty of music, right?

1246
01:03:32.650 --> 01:03:38.190
The song that like would make me tear up by the end is a lefty Feazelle song called

1247
01:03:38.750 --> 01:03:40.090
That's the Way Love Goes.

1248
01:03:42.370 --> 01:03:44.450
So and yeah, it's an old song.

1249
01:03:44.610 --> 01:03:45.450
Some of you will know it.

1250
01:03:45.630 --> 01:03:51.090
Some of you in this moment know that I'm 100 years old because it's a really old song.

1251
01:03:51.270 --> 01:03:56.110
Well, one of the songs I was listening to to help make me cry for Pam was a Ben Folds song.

1252
01:03:56.110 --> 01:04:02.530
But I had a whole Pam soundtrack that I would listen to on my drive to work during season

1253
01:04:02.530 --> 01:04:04.630
three when her and Jim were estranged.

1254
01:04:05.070 --> 01:04:07.550
And it was Nora Jones.

1255
01:04:08.870 --> 01:04:09.450
Lady.

1256
01:04:09.810 --> 01:04:15.710
She's just so soulful and wishing and a little loSt.

1257
01:04:16.010 --> 01:04:20.590
I remember you guys doing the scene and I remember it from the filter of your BFF.

1258
01:04:20.950 --> 01:04:23.230
And I remember you were really anxious about it.

1259
01:04:23.310 --> 01:04:24.810
You really wanted to get it right.

1260
01:04:24.810 --> 01:04:26.650
You were doing all the prep.

1261
01:04:26.910 --> 01:04:30.850
I remember when you guys went to do the scene that I left you alone.

1262
01:04:31.210 --> 01:04:34.650
You know, you don't need your chatty five foot one.

1263
01:04:36.310 --> 01:04:39.250
I was like, I'm gonna give her all the space she needs.

1264
01:04:39.450 --> 01:04:41.930
And rewatching it, Jenna, it made me tear up.

1265
01:04:42.030 --> 01:04:42.870
It's so beautiful.

1266
01:04:43.070 --> 01:04:46.690
And I think I see Rain as Dwight tearing up.

1267
01:04:46.970 --> 01:04:48.930
But surely Dwight wouldn't tear up.

1268
01:04:49.010 --> 01:04:53.310
But I think Rain, your friend, is tearing up watching you cry.

1269
01:04:54.570 --> 01:05:08.080
Angela, it's making me get choked up when you say it because this is a little bit of a place where the line blurred between the Rain that you and I know and Dwight.

1270
01:05:09.260 --> 01:05:13.260
Rain simply couldn't do that scene without empathizing with Pam.

1271
01:05:13.920 --> 01:05:18.320
Every time we did a take, he teared up when I cried harder.

1272
01:05:18.620 --> 01:05:25.880
It was like Rain Wilson, the man, was incapable of sitting next to a person in pain without feeling their pain.

1273
01:05:26.020 --> 01:05:27.840
And this is the thing that we know about Rain.

1274
01:05:28.000 --> 01:05:29.480
It's the thing that we love about Rain.

1275
01:05:29.480 --> 01:05:38.220
And when I saw it in the scene, it just made me miss Rain so much because that's who Rain is to me on a personal level.

1276
01:05:38.720 --> 01:05:38.880
Yeah.

1277
01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:39.880
Yeah.

1278
01:05:39.880 --> 01:05:41.060
I just saw Rain.

1279
01:05:42.040 --> 01:05:44.460
I just saw him and he's tearing up.

1280
01:05:44.460 --> 01:05:51.560
And then he has to snap back to Dwight, you know, and say the line or, you know, PMSing pretty hard, right?

1281
01:05:52.300 --> 01:05:52.820
Yes.

1282
01:05:53.560 --> 01:05:59.000
One of my favorite things he does in this scene as Dwight is he takes his jacket off.

1283
01:05:59.100 --> 01:06:01.460
And you're positive he's going to wrap it around Pam.

1284
01:06:01.560 --> 01:06:05.580
And he ties it around his waist so awkwardly.

1285
01:06:06.520 --> 01:06:08.240
Yes, that was in the script.

1286
01:06:08.240 --> 01:06:09.500
That's the other thing.

1287
01:06:09.740 --> 01:06:18.240
All the little beats, all the moments, him putting his jacket around his waist, giving the handkerchief, awkwardly putting his arm around her shoulder.

1288
01:06:18.460 --> 01:06:19.580
All of that is scripted.

1289
01:06:19.680 --> 01:06:20.400
All those moments.

1290
01:06:20.560 --> 01:06:22.860
It's just, it was a beautifully written scene.

1291
01:06:22.980 --> 01:06:26.700
And they gave us great time to figure it out and shoot it.

1292
01:06:26.800 --> 01:06:28.260
I'm really proud of that scene.

1293
01:06:28.380 --> 01:06:30.880
It's one of my favorites from the series that I got to do.

1294
01:06:31.120 --> 01:06:31.480
I know.

1295
01:06:31.620 --> 01:06:33.320
You just did it so well.

1296
01:06:33.520 --> 01:06:34.680
Where are we now?

1297
01:06:35.480 --> 01:06:37.160
Well, Jan has arrived.

1298
01:06:37.160 --> 01:06:39.160
Oh, it's so awkward.

1299
01:06:39.680 --> 01:06:41.080
Jan has arrived.

1300
01:06:41.140 --> 01:06:44.540
And you could cut the tension.

1301
01:06:44.740 --> 01:06:49.480
Even though we were acting, I remember Melora has such a presence.

1302
01:06:49.480 --> 01:06:53.940
And when she walked into that warehouse, it was like you could hear a pin drop.

1303
01:06:53.940 --> 01:06:58.440
Even though we knew we were acting, it just was a powerful moment.

1304
01:06:59.460 --> 01:07:00.140
Yes.

1305
01:07:00.460 --> 01:07:06.680
And Michael's slow walk up the stairs was so good.

1306
01:07:07.380 --> 01:07:08.440
So good.

1307
01:07:08.840 --> 01:07:09.740
So good.

1308
01:07:09.920 --> 01:07:14.400
And then Jenna, when they get back to Michael's office, what a power move.

1309
01:07:14.560 --> 01:07:16.920
Jan is sitting in his seat.

1310
01:07:17.200 --> 01:07:23.260
He has to sit across from her where the other employees would normally sit.

1311
01:07:23.400 --> 01:07:24.240
It was so badass.

1312
01:07:24.480 --> 01:07:25.360
I so loved it.

1313
01:07:25.360 --> 01:07:30.040
So now in this scene, you don't know what's going to happen.

1314
01:07:30.320 --> 01:07:37.040
You're pretty sure that she knows about this picture and she has come to just lay into Michael about sending out this photo.

1315
01:07:37.140 --> 01:07:47.040
But she's either unaware of the photo or she doesn't care because she tells Michael this amazing speech.

1316
01:07:47.040 --> 01:07:48.160
It's so amazing.

1317
01:07:48.480 --> 01:07:49.960
It's so good.

1318
01:07:50.160 --> 01:07:56.560
She says her therapist thinks she has self-destructive tendencies and she should just lean into them.

1319
01:07:57.100 --> 01:07:58.960
Let's try that for a change.

1320
01:07:59.440 --> 01:08:01.860
Let's just go with the destruction.

1321
01:08:02.420 --> 01:08:03.340
I love it.

1322
01:08:03.460 --> 01:08:10.080
She says, in this past year, I have been through a divorce, an identity theft, an identity theft.

1323
01:08:10.300 --> 01:08:16.620
She's like, my ex-husband, you know, can't or won't share his feelings with me.

1324
01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:26.040
And then she says, maybe she will find happiness by lowering her expectations and following her instinct to be with Michael.

1325
01:08:26.380 --> 01:08:30.060
Everything in Malora's performance is perfection.

1326
01:08:30.100 --> 01:08:36.600
And then it is mirrored by Steve's perfect reactions to everything she's saying.

1327
01:08:36.720 --> 01:08:37.920
He is confused.

1328
01:08:38.260 --> 01:08:40.880
He isn't sure if he can be happy.

1329
01:08:41.020 --> 01:08:43.380
He's clearly, like, afraid to react.

1330
01:08:43.900 --> 01:08:46.860
Yeah, he's frozen because he's like, she's breaking up with me.

1331
01:08:47.000 --> 01:08:47.520
Wait, she's not?

1332
01:08:47.720 --> 01:08:48.680
Which she likes me.

1333
01:08:48.820 --> 01:08:49.780
Oh, but she's leaving me.

1334
01:08:49.940 --> 01:08:50.860
Who's this therapist?

1335
01:08:50.940 --> 01:08:51.740
What's happening?

1336
01:08:53.060 --> 01:08:54.760
All of those things are happening.

1337
01:08:55.200 --> 01:09:00.220
And then, you know, she grabs him and gives him a big sloppy kiss.

1338
01:09:00.420 --> 01:09:03.940
I wrote on my card, Jan, hashtag DGAF.

1339
01:09:05.200 --> 01:09:06.160
What's DGAF?

1340
01:09:06.300 --> 01:09:07.700
Don't give a f***.

1341
01:09:08.600 --> 01:09:13.080
Oh, because she knows the camera crew is there.

1342
01:09:13.080 --> 01:09:15.300
She is clearly over it.

1343
01:09:15.520 --> 01:09:18.860
She's like, I'm going to lean into this, just like my doctor said.

1344
01:09:19.040 --> 01:09:21.780
She says, meet me back at your condo in 15 minutes.

1345
01:09:22.080 --> 01:09:24.900
And then it's my favorite moment.

1346
01:09:25.240 --> 01:09:28.520
I mean, well, there's many favorite moments, but this is one of my favorite moments.

1347
01:09:29.180 --> 01:09:30.420
He is stunned.

1348
01:09:30.980 --> 01:09:34.940
And he looks at her and says, you complete me.

1349
01:09:35.220 --> 01:09:38.240
Like from Jerry Maguire, like that scene in the elevator.

1350
01:09:38.440 --> 01:09:40.000
He's like, you complete me.

1351
01:09:40.000 --> 01:09:42.180
And she looks at him and pauses.

1352
01:09:42.180 --> 01:09:44.620
It's such a wonderful pause.

1353
01:09:44.620 --> 01:09:46.700
And she goes, oh, God.

1354
01:09:48.500 --> 01:09:50.600
All of that was scripted, Angela.

1355
01:09:51.000 --> 01:09:52.200
It's so good.

1356
01:09:53.060 --> 01:09:55.100
It says that he says that line.

1357
01:09:55.260 --> 01:09:58.160
And then it says Jan pauses and then sighs.

1358
01:09:59.300 --> 01:10:02.080
But she's like, still going to meet him at his apartment.

1359
01:10:02.720 --> 01:10:02.860
Yeah.

1360
01:10:03.060 --> 01:10:05.860
That, oh, God is like, well, I'm in it now.

1361
01:10:07.980 --> 01:10:13.020
Lady, we got a fan question about this scene that so intrigued me.

1362
01:10:13.780 --> 01:10:14.800
Listen to this.

1363
01:10:14.920 --> 01:10:16.000
This is from Nilly.

1364
01:10:16.000 --> 01:10:24.540
Nilly said, Daniel Levinson and Roger Gould are adult developmental psychologists who have

1365
01:10:24.540 --> 01:10:31.260
contributed massively to the field, including the study and definition of the midlife crisis.

1366
01:10:31.940 --> 01:10:37.940
Is Jan Levinson Gould's name a reference to the fact that she is the epitome of a midlife

1367
01:10:37.940 --> 01:10:40.380
crisis or is it just a coincidence?

1368
01:10:41.000 --> 01:10:43.080
That is an amazing catch.

1369
01:10:43.080 --> 01:10:48.160
I think it's a coincidence because I don't think they knew that the character of Jan was

1370
01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:51.460
going to become what she was when we started.

1371
01:10:51.720 --> 01:10:56.460
So when they named her Jan Levinson Gould, they were not aware that she was going to spin

1372
01:10:56.460 --> 01:10:58.800
out and go down this road with Michael yet.

1373
01:10:59.060 --> 01:11:00.580
That that happened over time.

1374
01:11:00.600 --> 01:11:06.440
But I will tell you that I appreciate this fan letter because it sent me down a fabulous

1375
01:11:06.440 --> 01:11:11.840
two hour rabbit hole where I Googled Dr. Roger Gould adult development theory.

1376
01:11:12.260 --> 01:11:12.820
Fascinating.

1377
01:11:13.640 --> 01:11:19.040
I'd like you to know that you can also get a book called The Seasons of a Man's Life by

1378
01:11:19.040 --> 01:11:20.220
Dr. Daniel Levinson.

1379
01:11:21.260 --> 01:11:25.360
But all of their work was combined into a wonderful book by Gail Sheehy.

1380
01:11:25.520 --> 01:11:29.980
She's a feminist journalist and she wrote passages, predictable crises of adult life.

1381
01:11:30.360 --> 01:11:33.180
Did I buy all these books and I'm waiting for them?

1382
01:11:33.540 --> 01:11:34.960
Maybe I did, Nilly.

1383
01:11:35.200 --> 01:11:35.940
Maybe I did.

1384
01:11:36.540 --> 01:11:37.840
I'll report back.

1385
01:11:38.080 --> 01:11:41.420
But guys, if you want to go on the journey with me, you can Google it all.

1386
01:11:41.420 --> 01:11:42.420
It was really awesome.

1387
01:11:43.540 --> 01:11:47.240
Jenna, I love this so much because you and I do our prep separately.

1388
01:11:47.240 --> 01:11:49.600
So I don't know the rabbit holes you're going down.

1389
01:11:49.780 --> 01:11:50.560
You're doing that.

1390
01:11:50.560 --> 01:11:53.940
And I spent half an hour looking up different flavors of jelly beans.

1391
01:11:56.400 --> 01:11:57.680
It's all valid, lady.

1392
01:11:57.880 --> 01:11:58.160
It's all good.

1393
01:11:58.540 --> 01:11:58.880
And all important.

1394
01:11:59.160 --> 01:12:00.180
It's all important.

1395
01:12:00.340 --> 01:12:02.920
Well, you know, so we go back to the luau.

1396
01:12:03.160 --> 01:12:04.200
It's not going great.

1397
01:12:04.320 --> 01:12:06.820
People look like they're having a pretty lame time.

1398
01:12:06.820 --> 01:12:11.520
And there's a scene between Roy and Pam and he kind of cheers her up.

1399
01:12:12.740 --> 01:12:13.240
Yeah.

1400
01:12:13.620 --> 01:12:13.800
Yeah.

1401
01:12:14.060 --> 01:12:16.860
Roy says, remember how we were deciding where to go on the honeymoon?

1402
01:12:17.060 --> 01:12:17.740
You wanted Hawaii.

1403
01:12:17.740 --> 01:12:18.860
I wanted Mexico.

1404
01:12:19.080 --> 01:12:20.400
Well, I was right.

1405
01:12:20.440 --> 01:12:25.820
So I think he's sort of saying that Mexico would be better than Hawaii because this luau is lame.

1406
01:12:25.820 --> 01:12:32.040
I don't track his reasoning, but Pam laughs and I think she really needed that laugh.

1407
01:12:32.360 --> 01:12:32.480
Yeah.

1408
01:12:32.660 --> 01:12:39.260
This was the first moment where we show that like Pam is being comforted by Roy.

1409
01:12:39.480 --> 01:12:45.400
So all along we've been seeing Roy try to make these gestures reaching out to her and this one

1410
01:12:45.400 --> 01:12:50.040
lands and this was kind of going to maybe put Pam and Roy back on track.

1411
01:12:50.040 --> 01:12:56.900
Well, before we wrap up back from vacation, Jenna, I have one deleted scene.

1412
01:12:57.260 --> 01:13:03.360
It's a Michael talking head and I have to share it with you because it really sums up

1413
01:13:04.960 --> 01:13:08.140
Michael's Michael's state of mind as he comes back from Jamaica.

1414
01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:08.840
All right.

1415
01:13:08.920 --> 01:13:09.620
I got to hear this.

1416
01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:10.080
What is it?

1417
01:13:10.220 --> 01:13:12.480
So Michael is driving in his car to work.

1418
01:13:12.480 --> 01:13:18.720
It's his first day back to work from Jamaica, driving in his car talking head.

1419
01:13:18.720 --> 01:13:24.560
He says, you know how they say, take a chill pill in Jamaica.

1420
01:13:24.960 --> 01:13:27.900
I took one guy on the beach, sold it to me for $40.

1421
01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:32.760
And after I finished vomiting, I was more relaxed than I've ever been.

1422
01:13:33.140 --> 01:13:34.800
I lost six pounds too.

1423
01:13:35.260 --> 01:13:37.420
And that's how I want every day to be.

1424
01:13:38.960 --> 01:13:39.480
Yeah.

1425
01:13:39.620 --> 01:13:46.800
He wants to take a pill, vomit his brains out, then be relaxed and then have a pina colada

1426
01:13:46.800 --> 01:13:47.540
at three o'clock.

1427
01:13:47.940 --> 01:13:48.120
Yeah.

1428
01:13:49.480 --> 01:13:49.960
Amazing.

1429
01:13:50.280 --> 01:13:50.640
Amazing.

1430
01:13:51.040 --> 01:13:53.260
Well, guys, that was back from vacation.

1431
01:13:54.060 --> 01:13:54.540
Yes.

1432
01:13:54.800 --> 01:13:56.720
Thank you so much for sending in your questions.

1433
01:13:56.980 --> 01:13:58.620
And a big thank you to Kim Ferry.

1434
